WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.544 --> 00:00:03.795
She's Robin Hooded an arrow,

00:00:03.795 --> 00:00:05.380
which is where you shoot an arrow

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:07.173
down the back of another arrow.

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:09.801
It's actually really hard
to do with wooden arrows.

00:00:09.801 --> 00:00:11.970
It's a lot easier to do
with sort of modern arrows,

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:13.346
'cause they're a tube of carbon.

00:00:13.346 --> 00:00:14.305
My name's Jim Kent.

00:00:14.305 --> 00:00:17.934
I'm also known as Grizzly Jim,
and I'm a traditional archer.

00:00:17.934 --> 00:00:21.604
I've been shooting the bow
and arrow for 35 years.

00:00:21.604 --> 00:00:24.315
Today, we're going to be
looking at some archery scenes

00:00:24.315 --> 00:00:28.111
from TV and film and seeing
how realistic they are.

00:00:34.951 --> 00:00:37.787
He's got what we call a
cant on the bow there.

00:00:37.787 --> 00:00:40.123
I tend to shoot with a
little bit of a cant.

00:00:40.123 --> 00:00:41.958
It opens up the sight windows.

00:00:41.958 --> 00:00:44.127
If your bow is up straight like that,

00:00:44.127 --> 00:00:45.628
generally the thing you're trying to shoot

00:00:45.628 --> 00:00:47.255
is going to be hidden by the bow.

00:00:47.255 --> 00:00:50.091
So I tend to drop the bow
out of the way a little bit,

00:00:50.091 --> 00:00:50.967
which is what he's doing.

00:00:50.967 --> 00:00:52.761
He's clearly pointing one way,

00:00:52.761 --> 00:00:54.220
but you can see the arrow in his hand

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:55.055
pointing the other way.

00:00:55.055 --> 00:00:57.432
And a lot of people call
that the archer's paradox.

00:00:57.432 --> 00:00:58.975
The archer's paradox is the fact

00:00:58.975 --> 00:01:01.853
that the arrow is pointing off to the left

00:01:01.853 --> 00:01:04.731
when you're actually pointing
at the target at the right.

00:01:04.731 --> 00:01:05.774
That's the paradox.

00:01:05.774 --> 00:01:07.192
And it's the flexing of the arrow

00:01:07.192 --> 00:01:10.195
that makes it travel downrange.

00:01:15.283 --> 00:01:17.285
Could I kill this dude running away?

00:01:17.285 --> 00:01:18.203
Depends on the distance.

00:01:18.203 --> 00:01:21.790
I am only really accurate up to

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:23.750
probably about 30 meters.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:24.959
I'd get him eventually.

00:01:24.959 --> 00:01:26.377
See, I'd be zigging and zagging.

00:01:26.377 --> 00:01:27.921
I'd be serpentining.

00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:33.843
There is a type of archery called clout

00:01:33.843 --> 00:01:37.388
typically done by English longbow archers,

00:01:37.388 --> 00:01:40.767
which is long-distance shooting

00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:43.853
where you'd shoot at a flag in the ground

00:01:43.853 --> 00:01:45.522
100 meters, 200 yards away,

00:01:45.522 --> 00:01:48.024
shooting up, shooting
down, closest to the flag.

00:01:48.024 --> 00:01:49.776
So there are people that can shoot

00:01:49.776 --> 00:01:52.487
very, very well at long range.

00:01:52.487 --> 00:01:56.157
Medieval longbow archers
could be pretty accurate.

00:01:56.157 --> 00:01:59.953
The bow looked like it had
what they call pony limbs,

00:01:59.953 --> 00:02:02.622
where you've got the basic bow,

00:02:02.622 --> 00:02:04.207
and then you'd have another set of limbs

00:02:04.207 --> 00:02:05.458
that come off the front.

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:07.335
So that limb is attached to that limb,

00:02:07.335 --> 00:02:08.711
and that limb is attached to that limb,

00:02:08.711 --> 00:02:11.506
and that gives you way more power.

00:02:11.506 --> 00:02:14.676
So it would be perfect for that kind of

00:02:14.676 --> 00:02:16.761
killing-dudes-running-away
kind of archery.

00:02:16.761 --> 00:02:20.515
I'm going to give it a 9 for
"Game of Thrones," I think.

00:02:25.728 --> 00:02:28.606
Compound bow is typically

00:02:28.606 --> 00:02:31.526
sort of either a target
style or a hunting bow.

00:02:31.526 --> 00:02:35.238
Basically, you can adjust the
angle that the limbs are at,

00:02:35.238 --> 00:02:36.573
and if you wind them in,

00:02:36.573 --> 00:02:38.658
it gives you a little bit more power.

00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:39.742
And I think what he's doing there

00:02:39.742 --> 00:02:42.662
is maximizing his power
on his compound bow.

00:02:44.414 --> 00:02:47.584
Now, you can buy arrows
that come in parts.

00:02:47.584 --> 00:02:48.793
That's not how arrows work.

00:02:48.793 --> 00:02:51.004
That's just asking for trouble.

00:02:51.004 --> 00:02:53.506
There are points in an arrow that flex.

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:55.633
And if you are cutting an arrow into three

00:02:55.633 --> 00:02:58.720
and assembling it, it's not
going to behave like it should.

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:02.265
[laughs]

00:03:02.265 --> 00:03:06.311
That there, that's John
Rambo's grenade-tipped arrow.

00:03:06.311 --> 00:03:08.563
There was a type of
broadhead you could buy,

00:03:08.563 --> 00:03:10.607
and it came in a case like that.

00:03:10.607 --> 00:03:14.319
I think that's just the case
of a broadhead sprayed bronze.

00:03:18.489 --> 00:03:20.950
Shooting left-handed, so that's OK.

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:24.370
But I know later on, he
shoots it right-handed.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:27.999
The arrow flight on that is utter garbage.

00:03:27.999 --> 00:03:29.000
I mean, he's shooting it OK.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.961
He's shooting a compound off
his fingers, which is fine.

00:03:31.961 --> 00:03:34.714
Compounds tend to use a release aid,

00:03:34.714 --> 00:03:36.966
which gives you a much straighter release.

00:03:36.966 --> 00:03:39.761
I mean, this type of bow,
the further you pull it back,

00:03:39.761 --> 00:03:41.679
the higher the poundage gets.

00:03:41.679 --> 00:03:45.475
A compound bow starts
high and gets lighter.

00:03:45.475 --> 00:03:48.394
So you can hold it at full
draw for long periods of time.

00:03:48.394 --> 00:03:51.439
They're a little bit more
fast, a bit more powerful.

00:03:52.523 --> 00:03:53.608
No!

00:03:53.608 --> 00:03:54.901
Grenade-tipped arrow?

00:03:54.901 --> 00:03:56.027
Yeah, they do exist.

00:03:56.027 --> 00:03:58.446
Whether or not they're commercially
available, I don't know.

00:03:58.446 --> 00:03:59.656
Everything else looked really good.

00:03:59.656 --> 00:04:00.907
The way he was setting up the bow,

00:04:00.907 --> 00:04:03.159
the way he was changing his
poundage and everything,

00:04:03.159 --> 00:04:04.160
I like that.

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:06.704
And the fact that he shot the
arrow, it flew like garbage,

00:04:06.704 --> 00:04:09.290
but if you got a grenade
siting on the end of the arrow,

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:11.125
it probably would fly like that.

00:04:11.125 --> 00:04:13.378
So, weirdly, I think it's
probably quite realistic.

00:04:13.378 --> 00:04:15.338
I'm going to give "Rambo" an 8.

00:04:19.884 --> 00:04:20.718
[bell thuds]

00:04:20.718 --> 00:04:23.805
There's a huge tennis ball
on the end of her arrow.

00:04:23.805 --> 00:04:26.641
The more weight you put
on the front of an arrow,

00:04:26.641 --> 00:04:28.726
the less efficient it's going to be.

00:04:28.726 --> 00:04:31.062
It's just not going to go very far.

00:04:31.062 --> 00:04:33.273
Kate: Let me just fix
this. Give me a second.

00:04:36.526 --> 00:04:37.527
She has a little grabber

00:04:37.527 --> 00:04:38.861
or something on the end of that arrow.

00:04:38.861 --> 00:04:41.406
Again, that's going to
add even more weight.

00:04:41.406 --> 00:04:44.367
It's not a case of one arrow fits all.

00:04:44.367 --> 00:04:45.910
Arrows come in spine weights,

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:47.954
and that refers to the
stiffness of the arrow.

00:04:47.954 --> 00:04:50.581
And obviously certain bows
need a certain spine weight.

00:04:50.581 --> 00:04:52.583
So the higher the poundage the bow,

00:04:52.583 --> 00:04:55.128
the stiffer the arrow needs
to be, and vice versa.

00:04:55.128 --> 00:04:57.714
So by adding a lot of weight
to the front of the arrow,

00:04:57.714 --> 00:05:00.258
you dynamically weaken
the spine of the arrow,

00:05:00.258 --> 00:05:01.926
so therefore the arrow would be outspined,

00:05:01.926 --> 00:05:05.346
and it would probably just
veer off to the to the right

00:05:05.346 --> 00:05:07.432
and not hit the bell at all.

00:05:07.432 --> 00:05:09.267
You can shoot something called a blunt,

00:05:09.267 --> 00:05:10.893
which is a flat arrowhead,

00:05:10.893 --> 00:05:14.689
which would be for either dump shooting,

00:05:14.689 --> 00:05:16.607
shooting tin cans, that kind of thing,

00:05:16.607 --> 00:05:17.984
or small-game hunting.

00:05:20.903 --> 00:05:23.114
She's moving her arm up.

00:05:23.114 --> 00:05:25.658
And when you're shooting at distance,

00:05:25.658 --> 00:05:27.744
whether it's low down or high up,

00:05:27.744 --> 00:05:29.746
you don't really move your arm.

00:05:29.746 --> 00:05:30.997
You move from the waist.

00:05:30.997 --> 00:05:33.458
So you kind of pivot your whole body,

00:05:33.458 --> 00:05:34.959
not just your arm up and down.

00:05:34.959 --> 00:05:36.586
That's something I just spotted.

00:05:40.298 --> 00:05:41.174
[bell dings]

00:05:41.174 --> 00:05:45.511
The shot itself, not
very realistic at all.

00:05:45.511 --> 00:05:47.513
But her shooting is pretty good.

00:05:47.513 --> 00:05:48.514
We'll go 5.

00:05:48.514 --> 00:05:50.475
5 out of 10. In the middle.

00:05:50.767 --> 00:05:52.685
Ready your bows!

00:05:55.104 --> 00:05:56.564
Fire!

00:05:56.564 --> 00:05:59.359
You've got really long arrows there.

00:05:59.359 --> 00:06:01.944
It looks like she's only using
two fingers on the string,

00:06:01.944 --> 00:06:03.613
one above, one below.

00:06:03.613 --> 00:06:05.323
I've seen people do that.

00:06:05.323 --> 00:06:09.243
And she's got quite an overdraw there.

00:06:09.243 --> 00:06:11.913
She's got this explosive release,

00:06:11.913 --> 00:06:13.998
which, again,

00:06:13.998 --> 00:06:15.917
in order to make a good archery shot,

00:06:15.917 --> 00:06:19.295
you need to keep it concise and simple.

00:06:19.295 --> 00:06:21.214
Because something like that,

00:06:21.214 --> 00:06:22.757
you're not going to be able to repeat it

00:06:22.757 --> 00:06:23.591
time and time again.

00:06:23.591 --> 00:06:26.886
If you've got a simple
release that comes back,

00:06:26.886 --> 00:06:27.804
you can repeat that.

00:06:29.972 --> 00:06:33.393
You've got to be safe
with everything you film.

00:06:33.393 --> 00:06:37.563
And I imagine that bow was
probably a very real bow,

00:06:37.563 --> 00:06:39.899
but it was probably such low poundage

00:06:39.899 --> 00:06:41.734
and had such a stretchy string

00:06:41.734 --> 00:06:44.445
that it goes straight back to being

00:06:44.445 --> 00:06:45.905
a straight piece of wood.

00:06:45.905 --> 00:06:48.533
The distance between

00:06:48.533 --> 00:06:50.368
the lowest point of
the bow and the string,

00:06:50.368 --> 00:06:51.953
that's called the brace height.

00:06:51.953 --> 00:06:53.788
If your brace height is too low,

00:06:53.788 --> 00:06:55.623
your bow becomes very noisy.

00:06:55.623 --> 00:06:58.501
It becomes quite quick,
but it becomes very noisy.

00:06:58.501 --> 00:07:01.337
And you're really, really in danger

00:07:01.337 --> 00:07:04.465
of getting some real nasty
string burn on your arm.

00:07:07.051 --> 00:07:11.722
Shooting multiple arrows
off a bow at the same time,

00:07:11.722 --> 00:07:14.475
what happens is, all the energy

00:07:14.475 --> 00:07:16.727
comes from the limbs and
pulling the string back.

00:07:16.727 --> 00:07:19.063
And what that does, it
pushes the arrow forward.

00:07:19.063 --> 00:07:21.274
And the heavier the arrow
that it needs to push,

00:07:21.274 --> 00:07:22.316
the more energy it needs

00:07:22.316 --> 00:07:25.111
to sort of get that arrow
to where it needs to go.

00:07:25.111 --> 00:07:27.613
But if you're shooting
multiple arrows, essentially,

00:07:27.613 --> 00:07:29.115
the bow doesn't know there's
multiple arrows on there.

00:07:29.115 --> 00:07:31.200
It just thinks there's
a really heavy arrow.

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:33.119
Two arrows is double the weight,

00:07:33.119 --> 00:07:34.912
and the more arrows you put on,

00:07:34.912 --> 00:07:37.665
the heavier weight that
bow is going to need

00:07:37.665 --> 00:07:39.750
to push the arrows through the air.

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:42.211
And it basically just takes
all the guts out of the bow.

00:07:42.211 --> 00:07:44.130
We'll give "Wonder Woman"
a 5. They did all right.

00:07:44.672 --> 00:07:46.424
Curse this dress!

00:07:48.009 --> 00:07:50.928
In order to shoot well, you
need to be able to move.

00:07:50.928 --> 00:07:53.473
So I think the fact that
they've got her in a tight thing

00:07:53.473 --> 00:07:55.766
and she can't shoot properly,
I think it's brilliant.

00:07:59.228 --> 00:08:01.689
Archery is all about
keeping everything straight.

00:08:01.689 --> 00:08:03.941
The less influence you
can have over the bow,

00:08:03.941 --> 00:08:05.276
the better the arrow is going to fly.

00:08:05.276 --> 00:08:07.737
So if you're influencing the shot,

00:08:07.737 --> 00:08:09.280
the shot's not going to go right.

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:10.907
So if you can keep
everything nice and simple,

00:08:10.907 --> 00:08:12.408
which she's doing, she's coming back

00:08:12.408 --> 00:08:14.368
on the same plane as the
arrow's going forward.

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:19.749
Archery is all about being able

00:08:19.749 --> 00:08:22.251
to repeat the shot time and time again.

00:08:22.251 --> 00:08:24.212
There's a famous book called
"The Art of Repetition,"

00:08:24.212 --> 00:08:25.546
which is all about archery.

00:08:25.546 --> 00:08:28.382
And if you can replicate your
shot time and time again,

00:08:28.382 --> 00:08:30.092
you will be a fantastic archer.

00:08:30.092 --> 00:08:33.304
She seems to have a nice
repeatable kind of form there.

00:08:37.141 --> 00:08:38.476
Now, look at this loose.

00:08:38.476 --> 00:08:40.269
The trick is, with archery,

00:08:40.269 --> 00:08:41.687
you don't let go of the string.

00:08:41.687 --> 00:08:45.650
The trick is to try and no
longer be holding the string.

00:08:45.650 --> 00:08:49.362
So you've got to relax your
hand, let the hand flow back.

00:08:49.362 --> 00:08:51.405
If you're kind of trying to open your hand

00:08:51.405 --> 00:08:54.033
as hard as you can, you'll
never beat the string.

00:08:54.033 --> 00:08:55.243
The string is going to be quicker

00:08:55.243 --> 00:08:57.245
than your reactions across the board.

00:08:57.245 --> 00:08:59.455
So if you can just get your anchor point

00:08:59.455 --> 00:09:01.541
and then relax your hand
and let it come back,

00:09:01.541 --> 00:09:03.209
you're going to get a
much smoother release,

00:09:03.209 --> 00:09:06.087
and you can see her hand just relaxes.

00:09:09.340 --> 00:09:12.760
A lot of people think arrows
fly straight. They don't.

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:14.804
If you watch an arrow in slow motion,

00:09:14.804 --> 00:09:17.557
it's like a wet noodle going downrange.

00:09:19.559 --> 00:09:20.810
She's Robin Hooded an arrow,

00:09:20.810 --> 00:09:22.395
which is where you shoot an arrow

00:09:22.395 --> 00:09:23.896
down the back of another arrow.

00:09:23.896 --> 00:09:25.565
And the grain tends to split,

00:09:25.565 --> 00:09:28.067
but the grain never
runs perfectly parallel

00:09:28.067 --> 00:09:30.778
down the back of an arrow. It
will always kind of peel off.

00:09:30.778 --> 00:09:33.447
It's actually really hard
to do with wooden arrows.

00:09:33.447 --> 00:09:36.492
It's a lot easier to do
with sort of modern arrows,

00:09:36.492 --> 00:09:39.287
'cause they're a tube of
carbon, and it's easier

00:09:39.287 --> 00:09:42.123
to kind of put an arrow down
the back of another one.

00:09:42.123 --> 00:09:43.291
That's 10.

00:09:43.291 --> 00:09:46.210
One of the best representations

00:09:46.210 --> 00:09:50.756
of the way an arrow and
a bow work together.

00:09:51.507 --> 00:09:54.343
Stark, got a lot of
strays sniffing your tail.

00:09:54.343 --> 00:09:55.636
Trying to work out what bow that is.

00:09:55.636 --> 00:09:56.846
Obviously he's got some gadgets on it.

00:09:56.846 --> 00:10:01.684
That could be a modified
Hoyt Buffalo sprayed black.

00:10:01.684 --> 00:10:04.895
He's using a finger and thumb draw,

00:10:04.895 --> 00:10:07.189
which isn't really necessarily

00:10:07.189 --> 00:10:09.150
going to work for that kind of thing.

00:10:09.150 --> 00:10:10.693
Hawkeye, that's a bit of a mess, dude.

00:10:10.693 --> 00:10:12.653
He's got a shooting glove,
which is good to see.

00:10:12.653 --> 00:10:15.323
He's got a couple of
armguards on there as well.

00:10:15.323 --> 00:10:17.074
That's what's called a Beiter armguard.

00:10:17.074 --> 00:10:19.035
That's just a make of armguard.

00:10:19.035 --> 00:10:20.536
Thin strip of plastic,

00:10:20.536 --> 00:10:23.998
and it just protects you from the string

00:10:23.998 --> 00:10:27.460
as it sort of vibrates back
to where it's supposed to go.

00:10:27.460 --> 00:10:30.296
Sometimes it can just catch your arm

00:10:30.296 --> 00:10:31.422
and just give you a little sting.

00:10:31.422 --> 00:10:33.299
So you tend to have a
little bit of an armguard.

00:10:33.299 --> 00:10:34.175
It just protects you.

00:10:34.175 --> 00:10:35.760
Traditional archers tend to wear

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:37.928
a bit more of a leather kind of bracer.

00:10:37.928 --> 00:10:39.305
You wouldn't wear two

00:10:39.305 --> 00:10:41.891
unless you're doing
something really wrong.

00:10:41.891 --> 00:10:43.768
Well, they can't bank worth a damn.

00:10:45.061 --> 00:10:47.480
If you can't look at it, how
are you going to shoot it?

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:49.649
The trend with these
kind of dynamic things

00:10:49.649 --> 00:10:52.318
is that there's never
any kind of anchor point.

00:10:52.318 --> 00:10:54.695
It's always just pull it back

00:10:54.695 --> 00:10:57.073
and let it go from whatever
position you're in,

00:10:57.073 --> 00:10:59.867
which is fine, but you're not going to get

00:10:59.867 --> 00:11:02.703
that kind of level of
accuracy that he gets.

00:11:02.703 --> 00:11:04.705
Like, he's shooting moving targets.

00:11:04.705 --> 00:11:06.248
Now, there is a famous trick shot,

00:11:06.248 --> 00:11:08.125
but you're looking in a mirror.

00:11:08.125 --> 00:11:12.004
And I've tried it and um ...

00:11:12.004 --> 00:11:13.506
almost succeeded.

00:11:20.179 --> 00:11:23.683
He's got, what, maybe 36 arrows maximum

00:11:23.683 --> 00:11:24.934
when he first starts?

00:11:24.934 --> 00:11:26.477
It's like films with guns.

00:11:26.477 --> 00:11:27.645
You get people shooting,

00:11:27.645 --> 00:11:28.729
ch, ch, ch, ch, ch, ch,

00:11:28.729 --> 00:11:30.272
and they never seem to have to reload.

00:11:30.272 --> 00:11:31.565
But it looks like it's fun,

00:11:31.565 --> 00:11:34.402
so I'm going to have to give it 4.

00:11:35.069 --> 00:11:36.278
Too slow!

00:11:36.278 --> 00:11:38.197
Fast as you can.

00:11:38.197 --> 00:11:39.323
Again! Come on.

00:11:39.323 --> 00:11:43.119
People can knock arrows out of the air.

00:11:43.119 --> 00:11:45.162
It's not something I
would recommend trying,

00:11:45.162 --> 00:11:47.081
'cause if you get it wrong,
you only get one chance.

00:11:47.081 --> 00:11:48.999
I want people to enjoy archery,

00:11:48.999 --> 00:11:50.626
but I also want people to be

00:11:50.626 --> 00:11:52.128
really safe when it comes to archery.

00:11:52.128 --> 00:11:53.879
There's a huge safety element to it.

00:11:53.879 --> 00:11:56.882
Although a bow isn't classed as a weapon,

00:11:56.882 --> 00:11:59.802
it's classed as sporting goods,

00:11:59.802 --> 00:12:01.095
it can be used as a weapon.

00:12:01.095 --> 00:12:02.263
He's shooting arrows at the guy,

00:12:02.263 --> 00:12:04.306
and he's knocking them out of the air.

00:12:04.306 --> 00:12:07.101
I'd hate for some kids to
think, "Hey, let's try that,"

00:12:07.101 --> 00:12:08.853
and do it and get hurt.

00:12:08.853 --> 00:12:09.979
It would break my heart.

00:12:13.816 --> 00:12:15.109
Try the other side.

00:12:15.109 --> 00:12:17.111
It will save you a second on the reload.

00:12:19.447 --> 00:12:23.117
He gets to show him a
different way of shooting.

00:12:23.117 --> 00:12:25.578
Now, this all came from
a Lars Andersen video

00:12:25.578 --> 00:12:27.163
that came out a few years back,

00:12:27.163 --> 00:12:29.707
and it kind of turned the
archery world on its head.

00:12:29.707 --> 00:12:31.584
And it suggested that you took the arrow

00:12:31.584 --> 00:12:34.712
from the left side of the bow
to the right side of the bow,

00:12:34.712 --> 00:12:39.675
which goes from essentially
a Western style of archery

00:12:39.675 --> 00:12:41.552
to an Eastern style of archery.

00:12:41.552 --> 00:12:44.597
Changing anything in
archery is a slow process.

00:12:44.597 --> 00:12:47.349
Takes time for your body to
get used to such a change,

00:12:47.349 --> 00:12:51.687
and going from one side
of the bow to the other,

00:12:51.687 --> 00:12:52.772
it would take time.

00:12:55.149 --> 00:12:56.692
Come on!

00:12:56.692 --> 00:12:57.902
Again!

00:12:57.902 --> 00:12:58.944
Faster!

00:12:58.944 --> 00:12:59.779
Yes!

00:12:59.779 --> 00:13:02.656
If you shoot the arrow on
the right side of the bow

00:13:02.656 --> 00:13:04.909
with a Mediterranean release,

00:13:04.909 --> 00:13:08.120
one finger above the
arrow, two fingers below,

00:13:08.120 --> 00:13:12.333
what happens is the arrow
veers wildly off to the right,

00:13:12.333 --> 00:13:13.375
which isn't ideal.

00:13:13.375 --> 00:13:15.044
And there are people that can shoot

00:13:15.044 --> 00:13:17.046
a Mediterranean-style release,

00:13:17.046 --> 00:13:20.424
like the way they get Robin
Hood to do in this film.

00:13:20.424 --> 00:13:22.760
Got to pull the bow out this way.

00:13:22.760 --> 00:13:26.013
If you're doing big, dynamic movements

00:13:26.013 --> 00:13:27.681
to try and get the arrow to go straight,

00:13:27.681 --> 00:13:29.058
they're very difficult to repeat,

00:13:29.058 --> 00:13:31.310
so you're not going to be
fantastically accurate with them.

00:13:31.310 --> 00:13:33.312
It's just something that I personally

00:13:33.312 --> 00:13:34.855
am not a huge fan of.

00:13:34.855 --> 00:13:36.941
There are people that shoot hybrid style,

00:13:36.941 --> 00:13:38.526
sort of East and West.

00:13:38.526 --> 00:13:39.443
At the end of the day,

00:13:39.443 --> 00:13:42.071
you've got to shoot in
a way that's comfortable

00:13:42.071 --> 00:13:43.906
and that puts the biggest
smile on your face.

00:13:45.282 --> 00:13:47.993
Because it does require a
certain amount of fitness,

00:13:47.993 --> 00:13:49.620
certain amount of stamina to shoot.

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:52.498
'Cause they're not easy to pull back,

00:13:52.498 --> 00:13:55.084
and you've got to hold
sometimes tremendous weights

00:13:55.084 --> 00:13:58.337
for a good few seconds
before you make the shot.

00:13:58.337 --> 00:14:00.673
You know, various lifting weights

00:14:00.673 --> 00:14:03.259
isn't necessarily going to
work the archery muscles.

00:14:03.259 --> 00:14:05.886
Faster, stronger, faster!

00:14:05.886 --> 00:14:08.305
That is actually a good archery
muscle he's working there.

00:14:08.305 --> 00:14:10.933
That's going to do more for
him than bench-pressing wheels.

00:14:10.933 --> 00:14:13.727
You can use stretchy
bands, things like that.

00:14:13.727 --> 00:14:16.856
That sort of builds up
muscles and whatnot.

00:14:16.856 --> 00:14:18.607
See, I want to give it a really low score,

00:14:18.607 --> 00:14:20.776
but I like the working out with the ropes.

00:14:20.776 --> 00:14:22.027
So I'm going to push it up.

00:14:22.027 --> 00:14:23.112
I'm going to give it a 4.

00:14:26.574 --> 00:14:28.409
She's using sound

00:14:28.409 --> 00:14:30.286
to make a deer move

00:14:30.286 --> 00:14:33.664
into a more accessible shot,

00:14:33.664 --> 00:14:34.999
which is fine, but I would imagine

00:14:34.999 --> 00:14:37.835
a deer would have just
bolted at that stage.

00:14:37.835 --> 00:14:40.337
From my experience, they're
pretty skittish things.

00:14:40.337 --> 00:14:42.631
Gale: What are going to do
with that when you kill it?

00:14:44.300 --> 00:14:46.176
I like it, personally.
When you're at full draw,

00:14:46.176 --> 00:14:48.721
you come to anchor, and you're there.

00:14:48.721 --> 00:14:50.764
If you're doing all this, the shot's gone.

00:14:50.764 --> 00:14:52.057
You need to have focus.

00:14:52.057 --> 00:14:56.645
She actually has beautiful
form in this film.

00:14:56.645 --> 00:14:59.189
And she was coached by an Olympic archer

00:14:59.189 --> 00:15:02.818
called Khatuna Lorig, an
American Olympic archer.

00:15:02.818 --> 00:15:04.862
Again, a fantastic archer.

00:15:04.862 --> 00:15:09.408
So her form is an Olympic form,

00:15:09.408 --> 00:15:12.411
which is fantastic for target archery.

00:15:12.411 --> 00:15:15.456
Now, she's in a hunting scenario.

00:15:15.456 --> 00:15:18.709
That type of archery isn't
necessarily going to work

00:15:18.709 --> 00:15:22.004
fantastically well in
this sort of scenario.

00:15:22.004 --> 00:15:23.380
There's so many variables.

00:15:23.380 --> 00:15:25.424
You're uphill, you're downhill,

00:15:25.424 --> 00:15:29.428
you're waiting at full draw for
something to come into shot.

00:15:33.432 --> 00:15:34.850
She was doing a bit of
wing shooting there.

00:15:34.850 --> 00:15:35.809
She was shooting a bird.

00:15:35.809 --> 00:15:38.687
Shooting a moving target
is also difficult.

00:15:38.687 --> 00:15:39.897
And you wouldn't be able

00:15:39.897 --> 00:15:42.816
to use an Olympic style of shooting

00:15:42.816 --> 00:15:44.234
to shoot a moving target.

00:15:46.070 --> 00:15:51.075
Now, they look like huge plastic feathers,

00:15:51.742 --> 00:15:53.452
and she's shooting off the shelf.

00:15:53.452 --> 00:15:57.373
That's not necessarily
going to work out too well.

00:15:57.373 --> 00:15:58.707
The sort of bow she's got there

00:15:58.707 --> 00:16:01.919
is not a million miles
away from this type of bow.

00:16:01.919 --> 00:16:05.172
That's called a shelf. So
the arrow sits on there.

00:16:05.172 --> 00:16:07.383
And as the arrow passes over,

00:16:07.383 --> 00:16:09.843
the reason why traditional
archers use feathers

00:16:09.843 --> 00:16:11.178
is because they compress

00:16:11.178 --> 00:16:13.889
and they don't really
cause too much of an issue.

00:16:13.889 --> 00:16:17.184
Where if you're shooting
plastic feathers, like those,

00:16:17.184 --> 00:16:19.186
they're not going to compress
like a feather would,

00:16:19.186 --> 00:16:22.982
and that's going to cause
some fouling on the shelf.

00:16:26.360 --> 00:16:28.696
Shooting a broadhead,
which is a hunting arrow.

00:16:28.696 --> 00:16:31.323
It's a two-bladed broadhead,

00:16:31.323 --> 00:16:33.409
and it stuck into the
target quite realistically.

00:16:33.409 --> 00:16:34.994
That's kind of how a broadhead

00:16:34.994 --> 00:16:36.954
would stick into a hard target.

00:16:36.954 --> 00:16:39.748
Even a light-poundage bow
would probably do that.

00:16:39.748 --> 00:16:41.917
It's not hardwired into the
pig's mouth or anything,

00:16:41.917 --> 00:16:42.835
it was just resting there.

00:16:42.835 --> 00:16:45.379
So I don't think it
would take a huge amount.

00:16:45.379 --> 00:16:48.841
I mean, I've tried to shoot apples before,

00:16:48.841 --> 00:16:51.093
and what tends to happen

00:16:51.093 --> 00:16:53.679
is the arrow just goes
straight through the apple.

00:16:53.679 --> 00:16:55.764
So you knock it off
whatever you're shooting.

00:16:55.764 --> 00:16:58.684
But I've yet to pin one to a wall.

00:16:58.684 --> 00:17:02.021
The type of archery I do, it's
called instinctive archery.

00:17:02.021 --> 00:17:03.480
I don't use a sight,

00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:06.025
and I let my mind and my brain

00:17:06.025 --> 00:17:08.944
and my muscle memory work subconsciously

00:17:08.944 --> 00:17:12.114
to let me know where to shoot.

00:17:12.114 --> 00:17:15.034
That's what she's doing
there. I would imagine she is.

00:17:15.034 --> 00:17:17.286
Although she's got a very Olympic style,

00:17:17.286 --> 00:17:21.123
where she's looking at
something and she's just, bang.

00:17:21.123 --> 00:17:22.499
And she nailed it.

00:17:22.499 --> 00:17:25.127
I've always got a soft spot
for "The Hunger Games."

00:17:25.127 --> 00:17:26.420
I'm going to give it a 7.

00:17:29.214 --> 00:17:30.424
A whole battery of archers

00:17:30.424 --> 00:17:33.093
is going to really mess some orcs up,

00:17:33.093 --> 00:17:34.845
which is what's happening there.

00:17:34.845 --> 00:17:37.973
I can't see the types of
arrow points they're using,

00:17:37.973 --> 00:17:39.600
but I see in a lot of films

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:41.852
people using broadheads
and shooting armor,

00:17:41.852 --> 00:17:42.936
and that wouldn't work.

00:17:42.936 --> 00:17:44.646
There were certain arrow points

00:17:44.646 --> 00:17:46.732
that were designed to go
straight through armor,

00:17:46.732 --> 00:17:48.817
like a bodkin. It's pointy,

00:17:48.817 --> 00:17:50.360
but it's bulbous, and it's stiffer,

00:17:50.360 --> 00:17:53.614
and it was designed to kind
of punch through armor.

00:17:56.575 --> 00:17:58.577
His elbow is a bit high,

00:17:58.577 --> 00:18:00.287
but, yeah, I'll give him that.

00:18:00.287 --> 00:18:04.041
When you got some big orc dude
with a stick coming at you,

00:18:04.041 --> 00:18:05.751
you're not always going to get a chance

00:18:05.751 --> 00:18:08.504
to set and make the perfect shot.

00:18:08.504 --> 00:18:11.590
But in order to speed-shoot
to that kind of level,

00:18:13.008 --> 00:18:15.636
you'd need a pretty light-poundage bow.

00:18:15.636 --> 00:18:18.055
I'm going to go 4, but it's cool.

00:18:22.101 --> 00:18:23.727
This is more of an Eastern style.

00:18:23.727 --> 00:18:26.271
His arrow is really short,

00:18:26.271 --> 00:18:27.397
and it's actually got, like,

00:18:27.397 --> 00:18:29.316
a piece of bamboo on the arrow

00:18:29.316 --> 00:18:30.359
attached to the bow

00:18:30.359 --> 00:18:32.778
where the short arrow will [whistles].

00:18:32.778 --> 00:18:34.655
'Cause obviously the longer
the arrow, the heavier.

00:18:34.655 --> 00:18:36.323
The shorter the arrow, the lighter.

00:18:36.323 --> 00:18:38.033
The lighter something is,
the further it's going to go,

00:18:38.033 --> 00:18:39.118
the faster it's going to go.

00:18:39.118 --> 00:18:41.120
So it means you can pull
the bow back all the way

00:18:41.120 --> 00:18:44.748
and put maximum, maximum
juice into that little arrow.

00:18:44.748 --> 00:18:46.291
He's using his thumb.

00:18:47.167 --> 00:18:50.754
Typically you overdraw
with that style of archery

00:18:50.754 --> 00:18:52.631
and relax the hand and
come away from the thumb

00:18:52.631 --> 00:18:54.508
and let the string [whistles].

00:18:56.718 --> 00:18:58.262
You can see the focus in his face.

00:18:58.262 --> 00:18:59.596
Breathing is a weird one.

00:18:59.596 --> 00:19:00.931
Some people hold their breath,

00:19:00.931 --> 00:19:02.266
some people breathe through the shot.

00:19:02.266 --> 00:19:05.185
If you're hunting and
you're waiting for a deer

00:19:05.185 --> 00:19:06.937
to sort of come into view,

00:19:06.937 --> 00:19:11.525
you could be holding for
a good 10, 15, 20 seconds.

00:19:11.525 --> 00:19:14.862
Olympic archers tend to
hold for a lot longer.

00:19:14.862 --> 00:19:16.572
Some people are even quicker.

00:19:16.572 --> 00:19:18.699
Some people come to full
draw and then make the shot.

00:19:22.452 --> 00:19:24.204
Guess if you can't drop him straight away,

00:19:24.204 --> 00:19:26.915
you can wound him.

00:19:26.915 --> 00:19:28.959
And then you can chase him down

00:19:28.959 --> 00:19:31.211
and poke him with something else.

00:19:31.211 --> 00:19:32.504
Obviously, if there was a barb on it,

00:19:32.504 --> 00:19:34.214
you couldn't pull it out.

00:19:34.214 --> 00:19:36.091
You'd kind of have to pull it through,

00:19:36.091 --> 00:19:38.427
which, I mean, would be agony.

00:19:38.427 --> 00:19:41.221
That to me looked like a sharpened stick.

00:19:41.221 --> 00:19:44.349
So, yeah, you could
probably pull that out.

00:19:44.349 --> 00:19:46.810
Medieval archers used to do,

00:19:46.810 --> 00:19:49.479
when they put their arrow points on,

00:19:49.479 --> 00:19:53.317
they would put them on with wax.

00:19:53.317 --> 00:19:54.443
If they hit you,

00:19:54.443 --> 00:19:57.988
you'd pull it out, the
point's still in you.

00:19:57.988 --> 00:19:59.865
Or if you missed, it landed on the floor,

00:19:59.865 --> 00:20:00.949
the point would come off,

00:20:00.949 --> 00:20:03.076
so it couldn't be shot back at you.

00:20:03.076 --> 00:20:06.663
But, weirdly, archers
nowadays are more likely

00:20:06.663 --> 00:20:10.083
to be injured by the nock
end than the pointy end.

00:20:10.083 --> 00:20:11.251
Because when that's in the target,

00:20:11.251 --> 00:20:13.545
people walk up and stumble and things,

00:20:13.545 --> 00:20:15.505
and that can be quite sharp.

00:20:15.505 --> 00:20:16.673
I'm going to give that one 9.

00:20:16.673 --> 00:20:17.507
Thank you for watching.

00:20:17.507 --> 00:20:18.467
If you enjoyed that video,

00:20:18.467 --> 00:20:21.220
then click up there and
watch the one above.

